@Node MoveFile()

zmf.library/MoveFile

   NAME
      MoveFile -- Move's a file to another place quickly.

   SYNOPSIS
      int MoveFile ( From, To, BufferSize )
       d0             a0   a1   d0

      Success = MoveFile ( char *, char *, ULONG )

   FUNCTION
      Move's a file from one path to the other, checking if
      the file's paths are on the same partition, and if 
      so, Rename()'ing the file instead of copying. This makes
      file operations much faster. If the files cannot be
      renamed, then they are copied, using a buffer of 'BufferSize'
      or less.

   INPUTS
      From -- The source file.
      To   -- The destination file.
      BuffSize -- The copy buffer in bytes (32768 = 32k)

   RESULTS
      1 - OK
      0 - Cannot Open 'From' file
      -1- Cannot Open 'To' file
      -2- Cannot Allocate Buffer

@EndNode

@Node ReadAddresses()

zmf.library/ReadAddresses

   NAME
      ReadAddresses

   SYNOPSIS
      List = ReadAddresses ( Text, IDString, Position )
       d0                           a0    a1        d0
      struct List *ReadAddresses ( char *, char *, ULONG )

   FUNCTION
      A very useful routine for processing SEEN-BY: lists of
      FidoNet addresses in a piece of text. For example,
      ---------------------------------
      ^APATH: 1/1 2 2/1 35 36 37
      ^APATH: 3/5 6 7 84
      ---------------------------------
      This function parses the text, and puts each address in
      a struct List, which is then returned. You can put what
      string each line begins with (e.g "SEEN-BY"), and what
      Position this text appears at, width-ways.
      If the IDString does not appear at Position, then the
      line is ignored. A line can be terminated by a '\n', '\r\n'
      or '\r'.
      The list structure will contain one or more 'struct
      ReadAdrNode's in it. The list must be freed after use
      by FreeAddresses().
      If you wish to sort the addresses, the returned list
      could be used in conjunction with CompareAddresses() by
      MySort(), a clever sorting routine in this library.
      Addresses can be added to this list using standard (Exec)
      List manipulation functions, e.g. AddTail().

   INPUTS
      Text -- Pointer to text for processing.
      IDString -- Text which appears at the beginning at each
         line.
      Position -- Characters in from the margin at which 
         'IDString' appears.

   RESULTS
      NULL - Error Parsing List.
      >0 - Pointer to a 'struct List' containing one or more
         address. A list will still be returned if no
         entries were processed, and must still be freed
         accordingly by FreeAddresses().

   SEE ALSO
      @{"WriteMessages" Link WriteMessages()}, @{"FreeAddresses" Link FreeAddresses()}
      @{"CompareAddresses" Link CompareAddresses()}, @{"MySort" Link MySort()}, "include:exec/lists.h"

@EndNode

@Node WriteAddresses()

zmf.library/WriteAddresses

   NAME
      WriteAddresses

   SYNOPSIS
      Buffer = WriteAddresses ( AddressList, WrapLen, Flags, IDString )
       d0                         a0           d0       d1 
      char *WriteAddresses ( struct List *, ULONG, int, char * )

   FUNCTION
      Write's a list of addresses into a buffer allocated by
      the function. This function can write the addresses in
      several different formats, and will be word-wrapped by
      the length specified in Flags. This function may fail
      if the amount of memory required cannot be allocated, and
      will return NULL if this is the case. The pointer
      returned by WriteAddresses() is AllocVec'd, so, when you
      have finished using the data, you should FreeVec() the
      returned pointer, so as to free all the data used by
      the function.
      If you do not want an IDString to be output at the
      beginning of each line, then set this argument to "", not
      NULL!   

   INPUTS
      List -- A list containing one or more 'struct ReadAdrNode'
              entries.
      WrapLen -- Length to word-wrap the text at.
      Flags -- Format in which the addresses will be output
               in
               #define FIDO_STANDARD_N  1
               #define FIDO_STANDARD_R  2
                         #define FIDO_STANDARD_RN 3
               where 'N' means that each line will end with a '\n'
               character and 'R' means that each line will end with
               a '\r' carriage return character.
      IDString -- Character Pointer to String which starts each
                  new line, e.g. "SEEN-BY: "

   RESULTS
      NULL - Required amount of memory could not be allocated.
      >0   - Pointer to Buffer containing written text.

   EXAMPLE
      -----------------------------------------------
      /* converts paths to seen-by's */
      struct List    *list;
      char      *cp;
      char      *text; /* text has standard FidoNet message text in it */
      if ( list = ReadAddresses ( text, "PATH: ", 0 ) )
      {
         cp = WriteAddresses ( list, 75, FIDO_STANDARD_N, "SEEN-BY: " ) ;
         if ( cp )
         {
            /* cp contains buffer with seen-by's in */
            PutStr ( cp ) ;
            /* that's all I need cp for, so free it */
            FreeVec ( cp ) ;
         }
         else
         {
            /* writeaddresses failed */
            PutStr ( "Out or Memory!\n" ) ;
         }
         FreeAddresses ( list ) ;
      }
      else
      {
         /* readaddresses failed */
         PutStr ( "Couldn't Read Addresses from Text!\n" ) ;
      }
      -----------------------------------------------

   SEE ALSO
      @{"ReadAddresses" Link ReadAddresses()}, @{"FreeAddresses" Link FreeAddresses()}

@EndNode

@Node FreeAddresses()

zmf.library/FreeAddresses

   NAME
      FreeAddresses

   SYNOPSIS
      void FreeAddresses ( AddressList ) ;
                            a0
      FreeAddresses ( struct List * ) ;

   FUNCTION
      Releases all the data allocated by the ReadAddresses()
      function. This function may be called whether or not
      there are any entries in the list.
      This will also free 'AddressList' for you, so do not
      try and reference it after this function has been called.
      This function will never fail.

   INPUTS
      AddressList -- The list returned by ReadAddresses()

   RESULTS
      None

@EndNode

@Node CompareAddresses()

zmf.library/CompareAddresses

   NAME
      CompareAddresses

   SYNOPSIS
      Result = CompareAddresses ( First_Address, Second_Address )
       d0                          a0             a1
      signed int CompareAddresses ( struct FidoAddress *,
                                     struct FidoAddress * ) ;

   FUNCTION
      Compares two FidoNet format Addresses in strcmp format.
      Suitable to be used with MySort().
      This routine handles 5D (domain addressing) formats.
      However, if Adr->Domain[0] equals NULL then the domain
      will not be compared.
      The Zone field will not be compared if either equal 0.

   INPUTS
      First_Address -- First Address to Compare
      Second_Address -- Second Address to Compare

   RESULTS
      > 0 - First Address is greater than Second
      0   - Addresses are equal
      < 0 - Second Address is greater than First.

   SEE ALSO
      @{"MySort" Link MySort()}, @{"ReadAddresses" Link ReadAddresses()}, "Compiler information on
      run-time library function 'strcmp'".

@EndNode

